    $n$[["\nOr skip the `schedule` package entirely and let your OS handle the timing -\nsee the README for the crontab (Mac/Linux) and Task Scheduler (Windows)\nequivalents. Both approaches call the same run_briefing() function; only\nwho presses the button (this file, or your OS) is different.\n\"\"\"\n", "\nOr skip the `schedule` package entirely and let your OS handle the timing -\nthe guide's last step shows the crontab (Mac/Linux) and Task Scheduler\n(Windows) setups. Both approaches call the same run_briefing() function; only\nwho presses the button (this file, or your OS) is different.\n\"\"\"\n"], ["        print(\n            \"Missing SLACK_BOT_TOKEN or SLACK_USER_ID.\\n\"\n            \"Copy .env.example to .env, uncomment those two lines, and fill \"\n            \"them in - see README.md for where to find each value.\"\n        )\n        return\n", "        print(\n            \"Missing SLACK_BOT_TOKEN or SLACK_USER_ID.\\n\"\n            \"Add both to your .env file: SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-... (the Bot User \"\n            \"OAuth Token from Build 1) and SLACK_USER_ID=U... (your member ID).\"\n        )\n        return\n"], ["        # This only works while the process itself stays alive - if your\n        # laptop sleeps or you close the terminal, nothing runs. Cron /\n        # Task Scheduler (see README) survive both; this is the quick way\n        # to test the daily timing without setting either up first.\n        schedule.every().day.at(\"08:00\").do(run_briefing)\n        print(\"Scheduled for 08:00 daily. Leave this running... (Ctrl+C to stop)\")\n", "        # This only works while the process itself stays alive - if your\n        # laptop sleeps or you close the terminal, nothing runs. Cron /\n        # Task Scheduler (the guide's last step) survive both; this is\n        # the quick way to test the daily timing without setting either\n        # up first.\n        schedule.every().day.at(\"08:00\").do(run_briefing)\n        print(\"Scheduled for 08:00 daily. Leave this running... (Ctrl+C to stop)\")\n"]]$n$, '8fbe314d17152f9076eb40e3fddc936f'),
  (14, 3, 'session', '{builds,2,steps,0,verify}', '{builds,2,steps,0,verify}', 'str', '1a4ed36fb3b2fe24e8d970c72a28d27b', null,
    $n$Add two lines to the .env file you made in Build 2: SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-... (the Bot User OAuth Token from Build 1) and SLACK_USER_ID=U... (your member ID). Then replace CHANNEL_IDS near the top of main.py with your real channel IDs (right-click a channel name > View channel details, scroll to the bottom), then run `python main.py`. Within about 20 seconds you should get a DM titled "Morning Briefing - <today's date>" with Top Priorities / FYI sections, and "Briefing sent!" in your console.$n$, null),
  (15, 3, 'session', '{builds,2,steps,1,instruction}', '{builds,2,steps,1,instruction}', 'str', '9e39a834b409dc43cdc91f5c8b96fd7b', null,
    $n$Now set it up to run every morning on its own. Either run `python main.py --schedule` and leave that process running (it uses the schedule package to fire at 08:00 daily), or - better, since it survives a reboot - use your OS's own scheduler: crontab on Mac/Linux, or Task Scheduler on Windows (both exact setups are below - replace /full/path/to/project with your project folder's real path, which `pwd` on Mac/Linux or `cd` on Windows prints).$n$, null),
  (16, 3, 'session', '{builds,2,steps,1,prompt}', '{builds,2,steps,1,prompt}', 'add', null, null,
    $n$"# Mac/Linux (cron): run `crontab -e`, add this one line, save and close.\n# It runs at 08:00 every day and writes whatever main.py prints to briefing.log.\n0 8 * * * cd /full/path/to/project && /full/path/to/project/.venv/bin/python main.py >> briefing.log 2>&1\n\n# On a Mac, cron cannot read folders inside Documents, Desktop or Downloads unless you give it\n# Full Disk Access (System Settings > Privacy & Security > Full Disk Access > add /usr/sbin/cron),\n# so either do that or keep the project somewhere else in your home folder.\n\n# Windows (Task Scheduler): Create Basic Task > Trigger: Daily, 8:00 AM >\n# Action: Start a program, then fill in:\n#   Program/script:  C:\\full\\path\\to\\project\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe\n#   Add arguments:   main.py\n#   Start in:        C:\\full\\path\\to\\project"$n$, null),
  (17, 10, 'session', '{builds,1,steps,0,prompt}', '{builds,1,steps,0,prompt}', 'patch', 'a60fa803b535e2fc952c2a4e8b0f429b', null,
    $n$[["    That last one is deliberately vague about WHY it disappeared --\n    delisted, or your selector stopped matching -- because this function\n    has no way to tell those apart. See the README's troubleshooting\n    section for how a human tells them apart.\n    \"\"\"\n    changes = []\n", "    That last one is deliberately vague about WHY it disappeared --\n    delisted, or your selector stopped matching -- because this function\n    has no way to tell those apart. A human can: open the product page in\n    a browser - if it is still listed, the selector broke (the site's\n    HTML changed), so update it in main.py's TARGETS.\n    \"\"\"\n    changes = []\n"], ["\n    # A snapshot compared against itself must never produce noise. This\n    # is exactly the bug the README's troubleshooting section warns\n    # about (\"getting an alert on every run even with no real change\")\n    # -- usually caused by comparing raw scraped text that has stray\n", "\n    # A snapshot compared against itself must never produce noise. This\n    # is exactly the bug the guide's troubleshooting section warns\n    # about (\"getting an alert on every run even with no real change\")\n    # -- usually caused by comparing raw scraped text that has stray\n"]]$n$, 'f6b01b0bf2e13b23d47cb529fe244f5f'),
  (18, 10, 'troubleshooting', '{3,fix}', '{3,fix}', 'str', '821f1c502cd1da22a2fe0ce0ccf5fcd5', null,
    $n$If it's running on your own laptop, the schedule pauses whenever your laptop sleeps. For real always-on monitoring, schedule it with cron or Task Scheduler on a machine that stays on, or deploy it to an always-on host such as Railway or Render (check their current plans - neither keeps a job running 24/7 for free).$n$, null),
